New opt-in option `--refinement-renaming` (default off; `refinement-renaming = true` in a project file): a declaration in a refining module may rename what is internal to the declaration it refines (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/106, https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/107). A function, method, lemma or constructor may rename its type parameters, its out-parameters, its result, and the local names of its in-parameters; a class, trait, datatype, newtype or abstract type may rename its type parameters. The renaming goes by position, and the inherited specification, default values, body and members are renamed with the declaration. An in-parameter keeps its label, the name it was first declared with: a named argument `F(x := e)` uses the label in every module of the refinement chain, so callers are unaffected, and a named argument that uses a local name is rejected with `the parameter labelled 'x' is called 'y' only inside function 'F'; a named argument uses its label 'x'`. A renaming that would change what a name in the inherited code refers to is a resolution error that names both declarations (`a renaming to 'k' in refining module 'B' changes what the name 'k' refers to in code inherited from module 'A': ...`); so is a renaming of a name whose uses in the inherited code cannot be followed. The types, the number of parameters and their modifiers must agree as before. An iterator's parameters and type parameters cannot be renamed, nor the type parameters of an abstract type with members that a type synonym refines. The option cannot be combined with `--legacy-inherited-name-capture`. With the option off nothing changes: each renaming is rejected with the same errors as before. Independently of the option, a `match` pattern with a type annotation is now copied with its own type when a refining module inherits it; the copy shared the type of the refined module, which crashed the resolver or reported a type mismatch.
